@page "/docs/cli"
<PageTitle>CLI - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">CLI</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">dotnet tool</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">source you own</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p>The Blaizio CLI (<code>blaizio</code>) exists because the styled layer is <em>your source, not a package</em>. It copies components from a registry into your project, rewrites them to your namespace, resolves their dependencies (both registry items and NuGet packages), records what's installed, and wires the Tailwind toolchain - so a component you add today is a file you can open, read and edit tomorrow.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Install &amp; update the tool</h2>
        <p>The CLI ships as a global <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/tools/global-tools">dotnet tool</a>. Installing and updating the tool is <code>dotnet tool</code>'s job; <code>blaizio update</code> (its own section below) is different - it moves your <em>project's</em> packages and components forward, never the tool.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Install" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Global options</h2>
        <p>Every command accepts these. <code>--yes</code>, <code>--json</code> or <code>--silent</code> makes a run non-interactive (no prompts, defaults win). With <code>--json</code>, results go to stdout as machine-readable JSON and errors to stderr - built for scripts, IDE plugins and MCP.</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-c, --cwd &lt;DIR&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Working directory (defaults to the current directory).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y, --yes</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Skip prompts, accept defaults.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-s, --silent</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Mute output.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--json</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Emit JSON.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--registry &lt;URL&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Registry base URL or local path - overrides <code>blaizio.json</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p>Exit codes: <code>0</code> success · <code>1</code> error (also: <code>add --diff</code> found drift) · <code>2</code> registry error · <code>130</code> cancelled (Ctrl+C, or esc at any prompt). <code>blaizio help [command]</code> - or <code>-h</code> on any command - prints usage. Set <code>BLAIZIO_DEBUG=1</code> for full stack traces.</p>
        <p>Command aliases: <code>create</code> = <code>new</code> · <code>rm</code> = <code>remove</code> · <code>un</code> = <code>uninstall</code> · <code>list</code> = <code>search</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">From a solution root</h2>
        <p>Every command that works on a project also works from above it. Run it in a folder with no <code>blaizio.json</code> and no <code>.csproj</code>, a solution root say, and it looks for the Blaizio projects underneath (build output, <code>node_modules</code> and the like are skipped). One project found: the command runs there, with a <code>project</code> line saying which. Several: a list with every project checked; <kbd>space</kbd> toggles one, <kbd>a</kbd> toggles all, <kbd>enter</kbd> runs the command once per selection, <kbd>esc</kbd> cancels, each under its own header, with a tick-or-cross summary at the end and the worst exit code. A project failing does not stop the others.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/SolutionRoot" />
        <p>Each project keeps its own <code>blaizio.json</code>, registry, skin and ledger: there is no solution-level configuration, and the fan-out is exactly the command run in each folder in turn. <code>--yes</code> (or no terminal to ask, as in CI) takes every project. <code>--json</code> refuses several projects, naming them and asking for <code>-c</code>, because two JSON documents on one stdout is not JSON. <code>search</code>, <code>view</code> and <code>docs</code> answer a question rather than change a project, so they ask for one project instead of repeating themselves. <code>update</code> alone also lists projects that merely reference the Blaizio packages without a <code>blaizio.json</code>, and runs just its package bump there (<a href="#update">details</a>).</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio new</h2>
        <p>Starts an app from <b>nothing</b>: scaffolds the project from a template, then runs the whole wiring pipeline over it (config, packages, tokens file, host wiring, Tailwind) and adds the template's component set. One command, a running app. <code>create</code> works as an alias. Each verb means one thing: <code>new</code> starts an app, <code>add</code> wires Blaizio into the app you already have and grabs components, <code>apply</code> re-themes, <code>remove</code> takes a component back, <code>uninstall</code> leaves.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/New" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Template</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>What you get</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>showcase</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A complete WebAssembly demo app: layout with sidebar, command palette and theme toggle; dashboard, forms, overlays, data and auth pages; ~35 components pre-added. The best way to see everything working.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>library</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A Razor class library wired for Blaizio (Razor SDK, framework reference, packages, imports) - for building a shared component library. An existing bare class library is hardened in place.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>webapp</code> / <code>wasm</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Config, packages and styling are wired; the page scaffolding for these templates is still in progress - use <code>dotnet new</code> first and run <code>blaizio add</code> inside.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">a registry reference</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Any <code>registry:template</code> item - a name, <code>@@namespace/name</code> or URL. Scaffolds its files (tokens substituted), wires the project, installs its component set. A direct URL warns first: a template is source code that becomes your whole app.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Default</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[TEMPLATE]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>prompted</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>showcase</code>, <code>webapp</code>, <code>wasm</code> or <code>library</code> (see the table above).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-n, --name</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>directory name</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Project name for the scaffolded app.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-d, --defaults</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>off</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts; template defaults to <code>showcase</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-f, --force</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>off</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Overwrite existing scaffold files and <code>blaizio.json</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--style</code> · <code>-p, --preset</code> · <code>-ns, --namespace</code> · <code>-o, --output</code> · <code>--tailwind</code> · <code>--rtl</code> · <code>--pointer</code> · <code>--scrollbar</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>-</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Styling and wiring, passed through to the wiring pipeline. Same meaning and defaults as the <code>add</code> options below.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio add</h2>
        <p>Copies components into your <b>existing app</b>, wiring Blaizio in first when the project has no <code>blaizio.json</code> yet - one command does both. With no arguments it opens an interactive picker (<BzKbd>Space</BzKbd> toggles one, <BzKbd>A</BzKbd> toggles all, <BzKbd>Enter</BzKbd> confirms). The wiring leg (it never scaffolds anything):</p>
        <ul>
            <li>writes <code>blaizio.json</code> and installs the base NuGet packages (<code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, <code>TailwindMerge.NET</code>);</li>
            <li>creates the <b>tokens file</b> (<code>Styles/app.css</code>): the Tailwind input, your <code>:root</code>/<code>.dark</code> theme values with the chosen preset merged in, and the token → utility map - one file you own;</li>
            <li>gitignores <code>.blaizio/</code>, where the <code>Blaizio.Base</code> package materializes the static contract sheets on every <code>dotnet build</code> (generated output, like <code>obj/</code>);</li>
            <li>wires a Tailwind pipeline (see <code>blaizio tailwind</code> below);</li>
            <li>patches the <b>host page</b> the app already has - <code>wwwroot/index.html</code> (WASM), <code>Components/App.razor</code> (Blazor Web App) or <code>Pages/_Host.cshtml</code> / <code>_Layout.cshtml</code> (Blazor Server) - inserting the compiled stylesheet link and the pre-paint <code>boot.js</code> script.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>On an initialized project the wiring leg re-runs as a <b>top-up</b> whenever you pass a wiring flag (<code>--style</code>, <code>--tailwind</code>, <code>--rtl</code>, <code>--pointer</code>, <code>--css</code>, <code>-f</code>): the recorded config is kept, explicit flags override, and a wiring-only run (<code>blaizio add --rtl</code>) is a complete operation. Components carry their skin <em>inlined in their classes</em>: no skin stylesheet, no <code>style-*</code> class on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>. Page direction is never touched either - <code>--rtl</code> readies the components, setting <code>dir="rtl"</code> stays your call.</p>
        <p>Then, for each component the CLI:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>resolves its <em>registry dependencies</em> transitively (dependency-first order, each item once) and installs the aggregated <em>NuGet dependencies</em>;</li>
            <li>writes the files under your output directory and rewrites every namespace to yours;</li>
            <li>tops up <code>_Imports.razor</code>, plus a project-wide global using for the base namespace;</li>
            <li>records the item in <code>blaizio.json</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Existing files are <b>skipped</b> unless you pass <code>--overwrite</code> - your edits are safe by default.</p>
        <p>And even <code>--overwrite</code> will not silently discard your work. Every file the CLI writes has its content hash recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>, so a later run can tell <b>a file you changed</b> apart from <b>a file that simply has a newer version upstream</b>. Untouched files are replaced without a word; the ones you edited are listed with a checkbox picker (space to toggle, <code>a</code> for all, enter to confirm, esc to cancel) and only the ones you tick get replaced. See <a href="#local-edits">Your local edits</a> for what happens when nobody is there to answer.</p>
        <p>The registry is <b>checked before any work starts</b>. Wiring runs first and components are fetched after, so an unreachable registry would otherwise install packages, write your tokens file and edit the host page before failing - one request up front turns that into a clean refusal (exit 2) with the project untouched. <code>update</code> does the same before bumping packages. A registry that answers but ships no <code>index.json</code> is fine: items still resolve at the base path.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Add" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[COMPONENTS]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Names, URLs, or local <code>.json</code> paths. Empty → interactive picker. <code>name@1.2.0</code> pins the item to a version the registry stamps: the pin is recorded, <code>update</code>/<code>--diff</code> re-request exactly it, dependencies landing on a pinned name respect it, and a later plain <code>add name</code> unpins.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-a, --all</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Add every component in the registry.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--overwrite</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Overwrite existing files (default: skip them). Components you changed since install are asked about first, never replaced silently.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--prune</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Delete files in the output directory no registry item ships anymore (requires <code>--all</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--dry-run</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Resolve and report only - nothing written, nothing installed.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--no-deps</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Skip NuGet installs <em>and</em> transitive registry dependencies.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--no-nuget</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Skip only the NuGet install (monorepo / ProjectReference setups).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-o, --output</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Destination override for this run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-ns, --namespace</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Namespace override for this run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-p, --preset</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Apply a color preset as part of the add - the styling <code>apply</code> would otherwise need as a second command. A name (<code>nova</code> - the default, <code>aurora</code>, <code>comet</code>, <code>corona</code>, <code>eclipse</code>, <code>equinox</code>, <code>magnetar</code>, <code>meteor</code>, <code>nebula</code>, <code>polaris</code>, <code>pulsar</code>, <code>quasar</code>, <code>solstice</code>, <code>umbra</code>, <code>vesper</code>, <code>zenith</code>) or a <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">Themes</a> code, which can also carry a chart palette, radius scale and heading/body fonts - all baked into the tokens file as plain values; a webfont selection also links the Google Fonts stylesheet in the host page. Takes precedence over <code>blaizio.json</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--style</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Component skin for the wiring leg: <code>ash</code>, <code>aura</code>, <code>ember</code> (default), <code>flint</code>, <code>forge</code>, <code>glow</code>, <code>spark</code>, <code>wisp</code>. On an initialized project it only re-records - swapping installed components is <code>apply</code>'s job.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--tailwind</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Tailwind pipeline for the wiring leg: <code>auto</code>, <code>standalone</code>, <code>node</code>, <code>vite</code>, <code>rollup</code>, <code>postcss</code>, <code>none</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--rtl</code> / <code>--pointer</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Enable RTL support / the pointer cursor on buttons (wiring leg). RTL also installs <code>direction-provider</code> once - the cascade layouts flip with.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--scrollbar</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Thin themed scrollbars (wiring leg): appends the <code>scrollbar-thin</code> block to your tokens file, lighting up the marks the components already carry. See <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/utilities/scrollbar">Scrollbar</a>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-d, --defaults</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts in the wiring leg, all defaults.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--force-overwrite</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>With <code>--overwrite</code>, replace the components you changed too - no questions asked. Distinct from <code>-f</code> on purpose: discarding your file edits should not also reset the project's wiring.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y, --yes</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts. With <code>--overwrite</code> that means <b>your changed components are kept</b> - an unattended run never discards edits nobody was asked about.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-f, --force</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Re-wire from scratch (overwrite <code>blaizio.json</code>) before adding. Nothing to do with component files - that is <code>--force-overwrite</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--diff [path]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Compare installed files against the registry instead of writing (the optional value filters to one file path). Exits <code>1</code> on any drift - CI-friendly, like <code>git diff --exit-code</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--view [path]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Print file contents instead of writing (the optional value filters to one file path).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--css &lt;path&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Record a custom Tailwind input (bundler setups) - the Blaizio imports are kept in sync inside <em>this</em> file instead of the CLI-managed <code>Styles/app.css</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section id="update">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio update</h2>
        <p>Updates the whole stack <b>in lockstep</b>: bumps the Blaizio NuGet packages (<code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, <code>TailwindMerge.NET</code>) to this tool's pinned versions, then re-pulls components from your skin's registry variants - everything recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>, or just the ones you name - overwriting local copies. One command on purpose: components lean on the package's JS/CSS assets, so syncing one half without the other leaves them out of step (a component importing a <code>dist/</code> module its older package doesn't carry).</p>
        <p>No styling leg: the tokens file is yours, and the contract sheets version-track the <code>Blaizio.Base</code> package. A project still on the old <code>Styles/blaizio/</code> layout goes through the confirm-gated <b>v1 to v3 migration</b> first: components re-installed from the skin's inlined variants, the tokens file composed from your existing values, <code>Styles/blaizio/</code> deleted. Does not update the tool itself - that is <code>dotnet tool update -g Blaizio.Cli</code>.</p>
        <p>A project that only references the Blaizio packages - a <code>.csproj</code> with <code>Blaizio.Base</code>, no <code>blaizio.json</code>, a class library say - gets <b>the package leg alone</b>: the ids its <code>.csproj</code> already references are pinned to the tool's versions, and nothing is introduced or recorded. A solution-root run discovers such projects next to the wired ones, so no sibling stays behind on an older <code>Blaizio.Base</code>.</p>
        <p><b>Each entry is re-pulled from the registry it was installed from.</b> A plain name means this project's <code>registry</code>; a name recorded as <code>@("@ns/name")</code> goes to that namespace's registry instead. So a component that arrived from somewhere else has to be <a href="docs/registry/namespaces">recorded namespaced</a> - otherwise <code>update</code> looks for it in the default registry and stops with <em>"is recorded in blaizio.json, but this project's registry does not serve it"</em>. Record the source and reinstall it once (<CmdChip Command="blaizio registry add" /> then <code>blaizio add @("@ns/name")</code>), or drop it with <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove" /> if it is gone for good.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Update" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[COMPONENTS]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Components to re-pull. Empty re-pulls everything recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>; the package bump runs either way.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y, --yes</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts, so <b>every component you changed is kept</b> and the rest update. The safe shape for CI and scripts.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-f, --force</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Take the upstream version of everything, including the components you changed. The only flag that discards local edits.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--dry-run</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Report what would change - no package bump, no files written.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section id="local-edits">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Your local edits</h2>
        <p>Components are <b>copied into your project</b>, so editing them is the point. <code>update</code> (and <code>add --overwrite</code>) re-pull those same files, and the CLI keeps a baseline so it can tell the difference between a file you changed and a file with a newer version upstream: every write records the content hash of what it wrote next to that file's path in <code>blaizio.json</code> (<code>installed[].files[].hash</code>). Nothing else is stored, nothing leaves your machine, and line endings are normalized so a CRLF checkout is not mistaken for an edit.</p>
        <p>Four outcomes per file, and only the last one interrupts you:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>identical to upstream - left alone, reported as <em>already up to date</em>;</li>
            <li>untouched since install, new version upstream - replaced;</li>
            <li>you changed it, no new version upstream - left alone;</li>
            <li>you changed it <em>and</em> there is a new version - you decide.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>How that decision is made depends on who is running the command:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Run</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>What happens to the components you changed</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Interactive</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A checkbox list of exactly those components, with the changed files under each. Tick the ones to replace (<code>a</code> toggles all), leave the rest. Unticked keeps your version.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y</code>, <code>--json</code>, <code>--silent</code>, no TTY</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><b>Kept.</b> <code>-y</code> means "don't ask me", not "overwrite everything" - a scheduled update must never destroy work nobody was asked about. Everything else still updates, and the kept components are listed at the end.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>update --force</code><br /><code>add --overwrite --force-overwrite</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><b>Replaced.</b> The explicit "throw my changes away" switch, and the only way to lose them unattended. Two names because <code>add -f</code> already means "re-write <code>blaizio.json</code>".</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p>The same record answers a second question: <b>what upstream stopped shipping</b>. When a component renames or splits a file, the old copy would otherwise sit in your project still compiling, its type still resolving, and surface later as a puzzling "cannot convert" at some call site. An overwriting run takes those back out - only paths <code>blaizio.json</code> recorded, never a sweep of your output directory, and never one another installed component still ships. A file that still matches its baseline is provably untouched, so it goes; one you changed (or with no baseline recorded) is listed as left on disk and only <code>--force</code> removes it. It stays in the record either way, so <code>uninstall</code> can still take it back.</p>
        <p>A kept component stays flagged: its baseline is not moved forward, so the next <code>update</code> offers it again. To see what you would be giving up first, run <code>blaizio add --diff &lt;component&gt;</code>. Records written before this ledger existed have no baseline, so those components are treated as changed (listed as <em>no baseline recorded</em>) until the first run that rewrites them.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/LocalEdits" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio apply</h2>
        <p>Re-styles an existing project from a preset: the color tokens, plus whatever a <a href="themes">Themes</a> code carries (skin, chart palette, radius, heading/body fonts). Two modes:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Scoped</b> (<code>--only theme</code> / <code>fonts</code> / <code>tokens</code>): surgical value patches in the tokens file - your other edits stay.</li>
            <li><b>Full</b>: the skin swap, destructive by design - the look lives inlined in the component files, so every recorded component is re-installed from the target skin's registry variants (overwriting local edits; commit or stash first, and the CLI confirms before it writes), then the tokens are patched. This is the one place edits are <em>not</em> offered back to you: keeping a component would leave it wearing the old skin next to everything else, so the confirm covers all of them (<code>-y</code> accepts it).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A webfont selection patches <code>--font-heading</code> / the document <code>font-family</code> into the tokens file and wires the Google Fonts stylesheet as a host-page <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> marked <code>data-blaizio="fonts"</code>, so re-applies swap it and <code>uninstall</code> removes it. <b>Your fonts win by default</b>: when the project already defines its own typography (an <code>@@font-face</code>, a hand-written <code>--font-heading</code>, a document <code>font-family</code>, or an unmarked webfont link), a full apply skips the preset's fonts and says so - <code>--only fonts</code> is the explicit override.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Apply" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[PRESET]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A preset name (<code>comet</code>, <code>eclipse</code>, ...) or a <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">Themes</a> preset code (e.g. <code>32r</code>). Prompted when omitted.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--only &lt;parts&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Restrict to parts of the preset: <code>theme</code>, <code>fonts</code>, <code>tokens</code> (comma-separated). <code>--only fonts</code> also overrides a detected user font setup.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--pointer</code> / <code>--scrollbar</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Also wire the pointer cursor on buttons / the thin themed scrollbars into the tokens file - the same toggles <code>new</code> and <code>add</code> take, so applying a Themes selection to an existing app is one command.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio contrast</h2>
        <p>The safety net for a re-themed project. It parses the tokens file's <code>:root</code>/<code>.dark</code> values and measures the color pairs the components actually paint against <b>WCAG AA</b>, in both modes: every foreground-on-surface pair, the tokens used as text on the page background, the focus ring (3:1 non-text) and the button's derived dark fill. Failures list the pair and its ratio and exit <code>1</code> - CI-friendly, like <code>add --diff</code>. Primary's dual role (link text on the page vs a fill under its label) can't always clear AA with one value; those pairs report as warnings, never failures, and the default button derives an AA-safe dark fill regardless.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Contrast" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--all</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>List the passing pairs too, not just failures and warnings.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--css &lt;PATH&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Audit a custom tokens file instead of the recorded one.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--json</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Emit the findings as JSON.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio docs</h2>
        <p>Prints a component's metadata, its docs page URL and the full parameter reference (parsed from the registry source) - the API without scraping the website, for agents and IDE plugins as much as for people.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Docs" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio view</h2>
        <p>Prints a component's metadata, dependencies and full file contents - without writing anything. The read-only sibling of <code>add</code>: same item resolution, nothing touches the project.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/View" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio search</h2>
        <p>Lists registry components - from the configured registry, or the <code>@@namespaces</code>/URLs/paths you pass as extra positionals. <code>-q</code> filters by name/title/description, <code>-t</code> by item type, <code>--category</code> by the registry's category tags; <code>-l</code> (default 100) and <code>-o, --offset</code> page. (<code>list</code> remains as an alias.)</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Search" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio remove</h2>
        <p>Takes back individual components, <b>by record</b> like <code>uninstall</code>: exactly the files <code>add</code> wrote for each named item, plus its <code>blaizio.json</code> entry. Files you authored under the output directory are never swept up, and a file two components share survives while either is installed. Names resolve however you type them (<code>ColorPicker</code>, <code>colorpicker</code>, <code>color-picker</code>). It refuses to break the project: an item another installed component still depends on is reported and skipped (exit 1) unless you pass <code>-f, --force</code>. Packages and wiring are left alone - components that are now unreferenced, and NuGet packages nothing needs anymore, are listed for you to decide on. Asks before touching anything (<code>-y</code> skips); <code>--dry-run</code> previews. <span class="text-muted-foreground">(<code>rm</code> works as an alias.)</span></p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Remove" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio uninstall</h2>
        <p>The inverse of <code>add</code> (wiring and components alike), strictly <b>by record</b>: removes the tracked components and NuGet packages (exactly what <code>blaizio.json</code> recorded at install time), their <code>@@using</code> registrations, the tokens file when the wiring created it (an adopted file only loses the Blaizio import/source lines), the <code>.blaizio/</code> contract dir and its <code>.gitignore</code> entry, the standalone Tailwind targets, the host-page wiring (boot.js, stylesheet link, the Google Fonts link) and <code>blaizio.json</code> itself. Anything you authored - or referenced before the CLI ran - stays. Asks before touching anything (<code>-y</code> skips); <code>--dry-run</code> previews. <span class="text-muted-foreground">(<code>un</code> remains as an alias.)</span></p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Uninstall" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio eject</h2>
        <p>Copies the contract sheets <em>into</em> your tokens file and removes the <code>.blaizio/</code> imports - the styling plumbing is frozen at the current version and yours to edit from then on. Removes no dependency (<code>Blaizio.Base</code> stays for behavior and JS); it exists for when you want to <b>own</b> the generated layer instead of tracking it. Irreversible (a <code>Blaizio.Base</code> bump no longer reaches the ejected copy), so it asks first (<code>-y</code> skips). Prefers the materialized <code>.blaizio/</code> sheets (they match your installed package); falls back to the CLI's embedded copies when the project was never built. Add <code>&lt;BlaizioMaterializeContract&gt;false&lt;/BlaizioMaterializeContract&gt;</code> to the csproj afterwards if you also want the (now unused) materialization gone.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Eject" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio info</h2>
        <p>Shows the resolved project + configuration: tool version, csproj, namespace, output directory, theme, preset, registry and what's installed.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Info" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio tailwind</h2>
        <p>Blaizio needs Tailwind v4 to compile <code>Styles/app.css</code> into <code>wwwroot/app.css</code>. The <code>tailwind</code> subcommands inspect and wire that pipeline. <code>auto</code> prefers an existing setup (<code>vite</code> &gt; <code>rollup</code> &gt; <code>postcss</code> &gt; <code>node</code> &gt; <code>standalone</code>) and never clobbers a bundler you already have - even a bundler config <em>without</em> its Tailwind plugin yet wins, and the CLI prints the manual step instead of wiring <code>standalone</code> over it; on a bare project it picks <code>standalone</code>.</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Subcommand</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>tailwind detect</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Report which pipelines are present and which one <code>auto</code> would pick.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>tailwind setup --mode &lt;ID&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Wire a pipeline: <code>auto</code>, <code>standalone</code>, <code>node</code>, <code>vite</code>, <code>rollup</code>, <code>postcss</code>, <code>none</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>tailwind fetch</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Pre-fetch the standalone binary (CI / offline); sha256-verified.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Tailwind" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Pipeline</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>How it compiles</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>standalone</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><b>Zero Node.</b> Setup writes <code>.blaizio/Blaizio.Tailwind.targets</code> and imports it into your csproj - the native <code>tailwindcss</code> binary runs on every <code>dotnet build</code> / <code>dotnet watch</code>. The binary auto-downloads into a per-user cache on first build (or pre-fetch it, sha256-verified, with <code>tailwind fetch</code>). MSBuild knobs: <code>BlaizioTailwindVersion</code>, <code>BlaizioTailwindAutoFetch</code>, <code>BlaizioTailwindInput/Output/Exe</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>node</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Adds <code>@@tailwindcss/cli</code> as a devDependency plus <code>css</code> / <code>css:watch</code> scripts to <code>package.json</code> (package manager detected from your lockfile: pnpm / yarn / bun / npm). You run the install yourself.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>vite</code> / <code>rollup</code> / <code>postcss</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Detected and respected when already present - the CLI reports them but doesn't set them up. Vite uses <code>@@tailwindcss/vite</code>; Rollup and PostCSS-based bundlers (webpack etc.) host <code>@@tailwindcss/postcss</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>none</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Writes only the input CSS; compiling is yours to arrange.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p><code>tailwind fetch</code> options: <code>--tw-version</code> (a release tag or <code>latest</code>; default <code>v4.1.11</code>), <code>--musl</code> (Alpine builds; auto-detected), <code>-f, --force</code> (re-download). The cache lives under <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\blaizio\cache</code> (Windows), <code>~/Library/Caches/blaizio</code> (macOS) or <code>~/.cache/blaizio</code> (Linux) - override with the <code>BLAIZIO_CACHE_DIR</code> environment variable; one download serves every project.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio preset</h2>
        <p>Works with <a href="themes">Themes</a> preset codes - the compact strings that pack a skin, color preset, chart palette, radius, fonts and RTL into a few characters (e.g. <code>32r</code>). <code>new -p</code>, <code>add -p</code> and <code>apply</code> all accept them directly; these subcommands are for inspecting and sharing them.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Preset" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Subcommand</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset decode &lt;CODE&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Show what a code carries: skin, preset, chart, radius, fonts, RTL.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset resolve</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The inverse: read the project's recorded selections and print the code that reproduces them.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset url &lt;CODE&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Print the Create page URL for a code.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset open &lt;CODE&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Open that URL in the browser.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio.json</h2>
        <p>The per-project configuration, written by <code>add</code>'s wiring leg and kept current by every install. Commit it - the <code>installed</code> map is what powers <code>add --diff</code> and no-argument <code>update</code>, and each file's <code>hash</code> is the baseline behind <a href="#local-edits">Your local edits</a>: what that file looked like when the CLI wrote it. A file recorded as a bare path string (or with no <code>hash</code>) has no baseline.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Config" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Key</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Default</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>namespace</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>required</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Root namespace applied to every copied component.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>output</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>Components/Ui</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Where copied components land (project-relative).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>theme</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>ember</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The skin whose inlined component variants <code>add</code> (and <code>update</code>) install. Swapping it is <code>blaizio apply</code>'s job (components re-install).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>nova</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The color preset whose values are merged into the tokens file.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>css</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>null</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Custom Tailwind input for bundler setups. When set, the CLI keeps the Blaizio imports in sync inside <em>this</em> file instead of owning <code>Styles/app.css</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>cssCreated</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>false</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Whether the wiring created the tokens file itself - <code>uninstall</code> deletes it only then; an adopted file is yours.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>ejected</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>false</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Set by <code>eject</code>: the contract lives inline in the tokens file, and no command re-manages the <code>.blaizio/</code> imports.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>rtl</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>false</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>RTL <i>support</i> was requested at wiring time (the components mirror via logical properties). It never sets <code>dir="rtl"</code> - page direction stays the app's.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>https://blaiz.io/r</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Registry base URL, or a local path (resolved against the project). <code>--registry</code> overrides per run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>aliases</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>base: Blaizio</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Namespace aliases - <code>base</code> is the headless layer's namespace (drives the generated usings), <code>ui</code> mirrors <code>namespace</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>installed</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>{}</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Every added item and the files it wrote - maintained by the CLI.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <h3 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Changing your choices later</h3>
        <p>Nothing picked during the first <code>add</code> is locked in. <code>blaizio info</code> shows the current values, <code>preset resolve</code> prints them as a shareable <a href="themes">Themes</a> code; each setting has a command that changes it:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Setting</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Change it with</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Color preset</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply &lt;name|code&gt;</code> - patches the tokens file in place.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Skin</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A <b>full</b> <code>blaizio apply</code> with a <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">Themes</a> code that carries the new style - the look lives inlined in the component files, so every recorded component re-installs from the new skin's variants (the CLI confirms first; commit or stash before). <code>add --style</code> alone only re-records the choice for future adds.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Fonts, chart, radius</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply &lt;code&gt; --only fonts</code> / <code>--only tokens</code>, or install a font item directly (<code>add font-inter</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">RTL / pointer</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio add --rtl</code> / <code>blaizio add --pointer</code> - a wiring-only run is a complete operation. Turning one off again is a <code>blaizio.json</code> edit.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Thin scrollbars</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio add --scrollbar</code> - appends the <code>scrollbar-thin</code> block to the tokens file. Off again is deleting that block; restyling the bar is editing it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Tailwind input (<code>css</code>)</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio add --css &lt;path&gt;</code> - records the file and syncs the managed imports (and the token block, when missing) into it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Tailwind pipeline</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio tailwind setup --mode &lt;id&gt;</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Registry</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Edit <code>registry</code> in <code>blaizio.json</code> (or <code>registry add</code> for named extras); <code>--registry</code> overrides per run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Namespace, output dir</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No dedicated command: edit <code>namespace</code> / <code>output</code> in <code>blaizio.json</code>, run <code>blaizio update</code> to re-install into the new shape, then delete the old directory and update your own <code>@@using</code> directives. (<code>-ns</code> / <code>-o</code> on <code>add</code> only aim that one add.)</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio registry</h2>
        <p>A registry is a folder of JSON served over HTTP (or sitting on disk): an <code>index.json</code> catalogue plus one <code>&lt;name&gt;.json</code> per item with its file contents inlined. <code>add</code> accepts bare names (resolved against the registry base), full URLs, or local paths - private and third-party registries work with nothing more than <code>--registry</code> or a URL. Items carry a <code>type</code>:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>registry:ui</code> - components. A registry built by <code>build</code> ships them as per-skin <em>inlined</em> variants under <code>r/{style}/</code> (the index's <code>styles</code> list); <code>add</code> resolves plain names against the recorded skin's variant automatically.</li>
            <li><code>registry:lib</code> - shared helpers, like <code>utils</code>.</li>
            <li><code>registry:font</code> - a webfont selection (<code>font-inter</code> for the body, <code>font-heading-space-grotesk</code> for headings) that installs by patching the tokens file and host link instead of copying files. Generated from the same catalogue the <a href="themes">Themes</a> knobs use.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This docs site serves its own build at <code>/r</code>. The <code>registry</code> subcommands manage which registries a project knows:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Registry" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Subcommand</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry add &lt;@@NS=URL...&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Record named registries in <code>blaizio.json</code> (e.g. <code>@@acme=https://acme.dev/r</code>); install from them with <code>add @@acme/button</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry validate [PATH]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Validate a source <code>registry.json</code> manifest (default: <code>./registry.json</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio generate</h2>
        <p>For registry publishers: scans a component source tree (the folder holding <code>Components/</code>, the current directory by default) into a <code>registry.json</code> manifest - one item per component folder, dependencies between your own components inferred from the source, official components you build on named for you to add as <code>@("@default/&lt;item&gt;")</code>. <code>--fonts</code> appends the catalogue's font items, which only the official registry ships. The manifest is the input <code>build</code> compiles; <code>registry validate</code> checks one without building. <a href="docs/registry/author-a-component">Author a component</a> walks the whole flow.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Generate" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio build</h2>
        <p>For registry publishers: compiles a <code>registry.json</code> manifest into the servable output - per-item JSON with file contents inlined, per-skin variants under <code>r/{style}/</code>, and the <code>index.json</code> catalogue. Point any static host (or a plain folder) at the result and it is a working registry.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Build" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio mcp</h2>
        <p>Runs a <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol</a> server over stdio, so an AI agent can work the registry on your behalf: search items, read a component's sources and parameter API, install components (with their dependencies), and check the project's configuration. The tools answer with the same JSON documents the CLI's <code>--json</code> mode prints, so both surfaces stay one contract.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Mcp" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Tool</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>search_items</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Search a registry for installable items, with paging - the <code>search --json</code> shape.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>get_item</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>One item with full file contents and dependencies - what <code>add</code> would write, read-only.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>get_docs</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Docs URL, dependencies and the parameter API reference - the <code>docs --json</code> payload.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>add_items</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Install items and their dependencies as source. Files you edited are always kept, and installs from unrecorded hosts are refused - the trust prompt cannot be answered here.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>project_info</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The discovered project and its <code>blaizio.json</code> configuration.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p>Installing needs an initialized project: run <code>blaizio add</code> once in a terminal first, so the wiring decisions (packages, Tailwind, configuration) stay yours.</p>
    </section>
</div>
